<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege capability</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siege capability refers to the recurring pattern in which an empire's ability to capture fortified cities determined how far it could expand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">Siege engines</a>, sapping, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and later artillery allowed armies to take strongholds that would otherwise resist. Examples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>'s siege engineers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>'s use of Chinese and Persian specialists, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> artillery.</p></div>
